A||N||8||LoW_KeY||putting decal on window||||1||
Z||000000||LoW_KeY||11-16-2001||03:27 PM||||Ok I know taking them off is used with heat.. I had the directions but lost them, but I thought I'd throw the fiero symbol on my rear window how do you go about this? it's exactly like the formula decal material, thanks.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kc.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||11-16-2001||03:44 PM||||I'm assuming your decal has three layers - the waxy backing paper protecting the adhesive side of the decal, the decal itself, and some sort of protective pre-spacing tape across the front of the decal.<P><B>1.</B> Clean your glass well.<BR><B>2.</B> Measuring carefully, mark where you want the decal to go. <B>*NOTE*]</B> - measure to the decal itself, NOT to the edges of the pre-spacing tape - the edges of the pre-spacing tape may or may not be perfectly aligned to the decal itself, and you want the decal to end up in the right place - the pre-spacing tape will be removed after application, so it doesn't matter where it goes during the application process as long as the decal itself is aligned exactly and properly.<BR><B>3.</B> Use masking tape to make a hinge across the top of the decal (or any long, *STRAIGHT* edge of the decal) and tape the decal (with all three layers still intact) in the exact place you want it on the glass.<BR><B>4.</B> Lift the bottom of the decal up, 'swinging' it up on the masking tape hinge. Begin to remove the waxy backing paper from the decal, starting at the hinge, making sure that the non-adhesive side of the decal adheres to the protective pre-spacing tape attached to the glass. Be very careful not to allow the adhesive surface of the decal or pre-spacing tape touch any part of any other surface.<BR><B>5.</B> Make sure the glass is clean and that static has not drawn any particles onto the area where the decal will go.<BR><B>6.</B> Starting from the hinge, use a credit card or plastic application squeegee and begin applying the decal to the glass, using wide strokes all the way from one side of the decal to the other, moving down the decal a small increment at a time. This applies the decal smoothly while pushing all the air out from behind it.<BR><B>7.</B> Continue removing the backing paper as needed to continue squeegeeing the entire decal in place, keeping smooth tension on the decal to prevent wrinkles.<BR><B>8.</B> Once the entire decal/pre-spacing tape piece is squeegeed down smoothly, peel up a corner of the pre-spacing tape, being careful not to lift the decal itself from the glass while doing so. Gently peel the pre-spacing tape off the decal, leaving the vinyl decal in place on the glass.<BR><B>9.</B> Use the very tip of a brand-new X-Acto blade (or a fine-pointed sewing pin) to poke a very small hole in any visible bubbles and gently work the air out with your fingertips.<BR><B>10.</B> Step back and admire your handiwork...<P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000002||87fastback||11-16-2001||06:14 PM||||I will make this even easier for ya'.<P>1.  Clean glass with razor blade and glass cleaner.<BR>2.  Use a spray bottle with water and a few drops of "DAWN" dish washing soap - but only a few drops and spray the window completely so it is ALL wet!<BR>3.  Remove backing paper from decal and place on WET window<BR>4.  Squeegy all water and bubbles from underneath<BR>5.  Turn on defrost if you have it or just let it sit for awhile to let the rest of the water dry<BR>6.  Remove carrier sheet from decal carefully!<P>I am a professional tinter and use this same technique in tinting.  This guarantees no bubbles are underneath.  This is the ONLY way to apply decals of ANY sort to cars, windows tool boxes, etc...||||reg||1
Z||000003||87fastback||11-16-2001||06:17 PM||||Oops, forgot to tell you one thing.  Since you are placing the decal on a WET window with a slight amount of soap in the water, it will allow you to move your decal around into the desired position.  If it starts to stick a little while moving it, just spray some more soapy water underneath the decal.<P>The trick is to only use a few drops of DAWN soap though, more will take ALOT longer to dry!<P>Have fun and let me know if you have any more questions!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||11-16-2001||08:36 PM||||Just use Windex or any glass cleaner. Works just fine. Spray both the window and the back of the sticker. Let it dry a few hours or more before you remove the top carrier paper. <P><BR>========================================<P>The trick is to only use a few drops of DAWN soap though, more will take ALOT longer to dry!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sootah||11-16-2001||11:18 PM||||Why doesn't the soap break down the glue on the back of the decal? Also, I'm gonna tint my windows (after I get the damn car running, anyway) how hard is this to do? And by do, I mean PERFECTLY, any air bubbles at all will drive me totally batty and I'd probably end up punching the window out.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">       <BR><sup><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Black_Beauty_-_Front_Right.jpg" TARGET=_blank>85 GT</A> - <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Busted-Ass_SE.jpg" TARGET=_blank>86 SE</A> (Parts car)<BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||2
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||11-16-2001||11:44 PM||||"The only way"?<P>Damn, I guess I've been doing it wrong 40 hours a week for the last 6 years. Go figure.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>The wet way may work for window tint (which is vinyl-based), but the usual pre-spacing tape for vinyl decals is paper-based, so the margin for error is a lot less before the premask begins to deteriorate. Also, dishwashing soap is rarely needed for vinyl decal application, because it leaves residue behind the vinyl, and narrower stripes will end up not sticking.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Joe Torma||11-17-2001||11:22 AM||||Did someone say vinyl?<P>All the above procedures will work.  The hinge is the easiest.  Soap and water doesn't work that great.  If it starts to peel when removing the tape, squeegee some more.  Or let it sit overnight.<P>If you want the real application fluid, go to a sign shop and ask for it.  "Easy-on" is one type.<P>If you need some vinyl cut let me know!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||87fastback||11-24-2001||02:54 PM||||Sure all the methods mentioned WILL work, but some work better than others.  I have been tinting for A LONG time and I am just used to soap and water.  I do use a heat gun for EVERYTHING, including getting bubbles, dirt and other things out of tint.  It also works with decals to dry them faster.<P>I don't llike using glass cleaner since it dries TOO quick and you really don't have time to adjust the decal.<P>Use wjatever you like, just offering some insight what a professional glass shop has been doing for years.<BR>||||reg||1
